      "text": "To determine how many positive factors of 96 are also multiples of 12, we start with step 1 of the detailed solution. \n\nStep 1: Prime Factorization\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of the numbers involved. \n\n\\[\n96 = 2^5 \\times 3^1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n12 = 2^2 \\times 3^1\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Identify Factors of 96\nWe need to determine the factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12. Since 12 is a multiple of 96, any factor of 96 that is also a multiple of 12 will simply be $\\boxed{96}$ multiplied by any power of 2.\n\nStep 3: Find Multiples of 12\nAny multiple of 96 that is also a multiple of 12 will have the form $2^x \\times 3^y$, where $x$ and $y$ satisfy the constraints from the prime factorization:\n\n\\[\nx \\geq 2 \\quad \\text{(since (2 or 3) raised to any power is always an integer)}\n\\]\n\\[\ny \\geq 1 \\quad \\text{(since (3 or 2) raised to any power is always an integer)}\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Count the Factors\nTo count the factors, we consider $x$ and $y$:\n\n- For $x$, we have $5 - 2 + 1 = 4$ choices (since $x$ can be $0, 1, 2, 3,$ or $4$).\n  \n- For $y$, we have $1 - 1 + 1 = 1$ choice.\n\nBy the multiplication principle (combinatorics), we multiply the number of possibilities for $x$ and $y$:\n\n\\[\n5 - 2 + 1 \\times 1 - 1 + 1 = 4 \\times 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 4 positive factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{4}.",
